// PARKED REPRO (not in the suite — it compiles today, which is the bug).
//
// `D` redeclares `h()` with no `override`, and `B.h()` is not virtual. That is SHADOWING, and it compiles
// silently at any visibility. Which body runs is decided by the STATIC type, so `d.h()` yields 2 while
// `base.h()` yields 1 — exit 21 below.
//
// kama already rejects `public virtual` ("overridable method must be declared `protected`") precisely
// because a public override is a footgun. Silent shadowing is the same footgun with no keyword marking it
// — C# at least demands `new`. It should be an error: the ONLY way to redefine an inherited method is
// `override` on a `protected virtual` (may) or `protected abstract` (must). See ROADMAP §1.
//
// NOT covered by this rule: reusing a name that is PRIVATE in the base. That is not shadowing at all —
// the base's member is invisible to the derived type, so the two names are unrelated.
type virtual resource B {
    public fn int32 h() { return 1; }
    protected virtual fn int32 t() { return 1; }
}
type final resource D extends B {
    public fn int32 h() { return 2; }                  // silently shadows B.h() — should be an error
    public ctor make() { slot D r; return give r; }
    protected override fn int32 t() { return 2; }
    public fn int32 viaBase() { return base.h(); }
}
fn int main() { D d = D.make(); return d.h() * 10 + d.viaBase(); }   // 21 — should not have compiled
